# .dockerignore decides what "docker build" sends to the builder (the "build
# context"). Anything excluded here cannot end up in the image, even by
# accident, e.g. a careless "COPY . .".
#
# This is an ALLOWLIST: exclude everything, then re-include only the files the
# Dockerfile actually reads. A denylist ("exclude .env, .venv, ...") protects
# only against files you remembered; a new .env.production or a stray key file
# would ship silently. With an allowlist, a file you forgot to allow makes
# COPY fail loudly at build time, which is the safe way to fail.
#
# Excluded by the first rule, among everything else:
#   .env / .env.*          API keys - these must only ever arrive at runtime
#   .venv/ agentcrew_env/  local virtualenvs (~560 MB of Windows binaries that
#                          would not even run on Linux)
#   .git/                  full history, including anything ever committed
#   __pycache__/ *.pyc     stale bytecode compiled by your Windows Python
#   .pytest_cache/ pytest_temp/ .ruff_cache/   test and lint artifacts
#   data/traces/           local run traces (contain your questions and SQL)
#   data/*.db              the database is rebuilt inside the image instead
#   eval/ tests/ docs/ workflows/ .remember/ .claude/   not needed at runtime

# 1. Exclude everything.
*

# 2. Re-include what the Dockerfile COPYs. Later rules override earlier ones.
!requirements.txt
!app.py
!agentcrew/
!scripts/build_database.py
!.streamlit/config.toml

# 3. Re-exclude junk that can live INSIDE the allowed paths. These come last
#    so they win over step 2.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.env
**/.env.*
